{"qid":"Q2435816","names":["Sultanate of Harar"],"title":"Sultanate of Harar","endonym":null,"capsule":"After Adal's collapse, its surviving center contracted to the walled city of Harar, which became the seat of an independent emirate/sultanate. Under figures such as Emir Nur ibn Mujahid the polity fortified Harar and continued conflict with Ethiopia. It persisted as a distinct city-state successor before the Oromo migrations and later Egyptian conquest reshaped the region.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. This polity has not yet received individual source review: treat extents as indicative atlas convention.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Adal Sultanate","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Nur ibn Mujahid","nativeName":"نور بن مجاهد","roman":"Nūr ibn Mujāhid","start":1550,"end":1567,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Uthman the Abyssinian","nativeName":"عثمان الحبشي","roman":"ʿUthmān al-Ḥabashī","start":1567,"end":1569,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Talha ibn Abbas","nativeName":"طلحة بن عباس","roman":"Ṭalḥa ibn ʿAbbās","start":1569,"end":1571,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Nasir ibn Uthman","nativeName":"ناصر بن عثمان","roman":"Nāṣir ibn ʿUthmān","start":1571,"end":1572,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Muhammad ibn Nasir","nativeName":"محمد بن ناصر","roman":"Muḥammad ibn Nāṣir","start":1572,"end":1576,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Mansur ibn Muhammad","nativeName":"منصور بن محم